item.text:
Searches covered direct #WeCount files, GAO/inspector-general records for water, PACT and childcare, ESSER durability, disability access, sanitation enforcement, and congressional/oversight records for political conditionality. The direct April–December 2022 #WeCount report URL was recovered. New evidence materially strengthens post-Dobbs access, childcare reach/limitations, water-allocation controls, and the bounded conditionality check.

Still unresolved: I found no defensible evidence that first-year Bipartisan Infrastructure Law water allocations produced substantial completed-project or beneficiary outcomes during 2022; no independent 2022 PACT beneficiary outcomes beyond uptake/screening; no proof that the identified water, PACT, or ESSER weaknesses were corrected during 2022; and no substantiated national finding or enforcement action establishing partisan service conditionality. The congressional conditionality item below remains an allegation only.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"#WeCount Report: April 2022 to December 2022","publisher":"Society of Family Planning","publication_date":"2023-04-11","url":"https://societyfp.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/WeCountReport_April2023Release.pdf","claim":"Provider-level monthly data estimated 32,260 fewer clinician-provided abortions from July through December 2022 than would have occurred at the pre-Dobbs monthly baseline. Declines were concentrated in states imposing bans, while increases in states retaining access were smaller; telehealth rose from 4% of recorded abortions in April to 11% in December. The study does not capture all self-managed abortions, so it directly establishes severe geographic disruption in formal access rather than the total number of abortions obtained.","locator":"Executive summary and national findings; monthly and state tables for April–December 2022; Methods and Limitations. The accompanying April 11, 2023 release states the 32,260 estimate at p. 1, paragraphs 1–3.","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-C2-001","canonical_fact_key":"us_2022_post_dobbs_clinician_abortion_access_redistribution","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.3","6B.10"],"source_type":"independent_research_dataset","source_confidence":0.9,"source_confidence_reason":"Direct report from the research consortium collecting monthly provider data, with disclosed coverage, imputation, and limitations; it measures clinician-provided abortions rather than every abortion or every unmet request.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly measures April–December 2022, including the six post-Dobbs months in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Primarily a national and state-policy outcome under Biden, not wholly attributable to presidential action; it bounds the effectiveness of his post-Dobbs federal response.","contrary_evidence":"National aggregates obscure severe state-level losses, and increases elsewhere did not demonstrate that displaced patients could actually travel or obtain care; unobserved self-managed abortions limit inference about total incidence.","underlying_fact_key":"post_dobbs_2022_formal_abortion_access","lenses":["6B.3","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child Care: Observations on States' Use of COVID-19 Pandemic-Related Funding","publisher":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","publication_date":"2023-06-14","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-23-106833","claim":"GAO found that states used federal pandemic childcare funds for measures including waived family copayments and higher provider payments, but a full impact accounting was unavailable. Administrators interviewed in seven states in September–October 2022 reported workforce and supply shortages and uncertainty about what would happen when temporary funding expired; experts said inadequate subsidy rates left fewer providers willing to accept subsidies and therefore fewer usable places for families.","locator":"Fast Facts; What GAO Found, webpage paragraphs corresponding to lines 266–269 and 330–333; methodology at lines 337–338; full report pp. 4–12.","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-C2-002","canonical_fact_key":"us_2022_childcare_relief_access_and_sustainability_limits","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"],"source_type":"government_audit_testimony","source_confidence":0.92,"source_confidence_reason":"Independent congressional audit institution relying on agency records and structured interviews; the seven-state interview evidence is informative but not nationally representative, and GAO expressly found that complete impact data were unavailable.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Later publication specifically reports September–October 2022 administrator evidence and spending conditions affecting 2022 delivery.","ruler_attribution":"Biden signed ARPA and his administration oversaw the federal grants, but states implemented childcare policy and local provider supply determined realized access.","contrary_evidence":"Large expenditures and temporary administrative measures do not prove that families obtained durable childcare places; GAO identified persistent supply, workforce, payment-rate, and sustainability constraints.","underlying_fact_key":"arp_childcare_2022_reach_access_durability","lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"ARP Child Care Stabilization Funding Fact Sheets","publisher":"Administration for Children and Families, Office of Child Care","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://acf.gov/occ/map/arp-act-stabilization-funding-state-territory-fact-sheets","claim":"ACF reported that by December 31, 2022, ARP stabilization grants had reached more than 220,000 childcare providers and potentially affected as many as 9.6 million children. These are administrative reach estimates tied to supported provider capacity, not independently verified counts of children newly obtaining or continuously retaining care.","locator":"National summary above the state and territory fact-sheet map; reporting cutoff December 31, 2022; individual jurisdiction fact sheets for provider and child estimates.","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-C2-003","canonical_fact_key":"arp_childcare_stabilization_2022_administrative_reach","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.10"],"source_type":"official_administrative_data","source_confidence":0.82,"source_confidence_reason":"Primary federal administrative compilation with a precise cutoff, but 'impacted' children are estimated from provider capacity and do not establish new enrollment, attendance, affordability, continuity, or counterfactual access.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact cumulative reporting through December 31, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The grants arose from ARPA signed and administered under Biden, with distribution and service delivery conducted by state agencies and private or public providers.","contrary_evidence":"The total should not be described as 9.6 million verified beneficiaries; GAO separately found incomplete impact data and continuing shortages and sustainability risks.","underlying_fact_key":"arp_childcare_2022_reach_access_durability","lenses":["6B.2","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Clean Water: Revolving Fund Grant Formula Could Better Reflect Infrastructure Needs, and EPA Could Improve Needs Estimate","publisher":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","publication_date":"2024-07-15","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-24-106251","claim":"GAO established that 2022-era Clean Water State Revolving Fund allotments were governed by fixed statutory percentages dating to 1987 rather than a current assessment of population, affordability, and infrastructure need. Applying GAO's expert formula to 2022 data would have increased or preserved allotments for 31 states—by as much as 260%—and reduced them for 19 states by as much as 53%; nine states and the District of Columbia also omitted at least one noncentralized-needs category from EPA's 2022 needs survey.","locator":"What GAO Found, paragraphs corresponding to webpage lines 278–286; Why GAO Did This Study, lines 289–292; Matter for Congressional Consideration, lines 296–302; full report figures and tables on formula simulations using 2022 data.","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-C2-004","canonical_fact_key":"cwsrf_2022_statutory_formula_need_misalignment","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.7","6B.10"],"source_type":"government_performance_audit","source_confidence":0.96,"source_confidence_reason":"Independent GAO review of statutes, EPA and Census data, agency records, state interviews, and a seven-member expert panel; later publication specifically reconstructs allocations and needs using 2022 data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Later audit directly evaluates the statutory allocation system operating in 2019–2023 and runs its alternative formula with 2022 data.","ruler_attribution":"Biden's EPA administered the grants, but Congress—not the president—fixed the controlling state percentages. The administration retained oversight responsibility for needs-data completeness and downstream compliance.","contrary_evidence":"The fixed statutory formula weakens a claim that Biden nationally conditioned these funds on partisan loyalty, but it also shows that first-year allocations were poorly aligned with contemporary need and therefore cannot establish equitable access or effective delivery.","underlying_fact_key":"water_2022_allocation_controls_and_need_fit","lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.7","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Comer, Graves Launch Probe into Biden Administration's Infrastructure Spending","publisher":"U.S. House Committee on Oversight and Reform minority staff","publication_date":"2022-03-17","url":"https://oversight.house.gov/release/comer-graves-launch-probe-into-biden-administrations-infrastructure-spending/","claim":"Two Republican ranking members requested White House records and alleged that broad IIJA discretion might steer funding away from Republican-led states and rural areas toward progressive priorities. The release identifies a political-conditionality concern and oversight request, but reports no completed investigation, adjudicated complaint, enforcement action, recipient denial, statistical finding, or substantiated instance of funding conditioned on political support.","locator":"Release paragraphs 1–8, especially the allegations and document request at webpage lines 41–49; linked letter to White House Infrastructure Coordinator Mitch Landrieu.","provisional_id":"WEB-6B-C2-005","canonical_fact_key":"iiJA_2022_partisan_allocation_allegation_unsubstantiated","disposition":"discovery_only","chapter_ids":["6B"],"methodology_ids":["6B.7"],"source_type":"congressional_oversight_allegation","source_confidence":0.84,"source_confidence_reason":"Authentic primary record of what lawmakers alleged and requested, but it is not evidence that the alleged conduct occurred and contains no adjudicated or audit finding.","final_evidence_use":"discovery_only","period_fit":"Contemporaneous March 2022 allegation concerning infrastructure implementation during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"The allegation expressly targeted Biden administration implementation choices, but no underlying act by Biden or a substantiated national conditionality practice was established.","contrary_evidence":"GAO later established that major Clean Water SRF state allotments were controlled by fixed statutory percentages, while identifying need misalignment rather than partisan conditioning. The allegation must remain separate from findings.","underlying_fact_key":"iiJA_2022_partisan_allocation_allegation","lenses":["6B.7"]}
